| RL router (REINFORCE) | hidden / $K$ steps / lr / episodes | 48 / $K$ / $2\times10^{-3}$ / 3000 |
| Fabric partition (Sec. IV-C) | nodes (co-located + edge) / roles / capacity $b$ / seeds | 15 (3 + 12) / 42 / 4 / 5 |
| K3s prototype (Sec. IV-D) | nodes on two WAN machines / roles / per-node cap; detection | 16 / 64 / 6; grace-period $\approx$ 40 s |

Validation uses two platforms. The first is a decentralized container fabric built specifically to isolate the loss-of-control-plane regime: each node is an agent that holds a set of roles under a capacity limit $b$; two virtual networks separate the control channel (agent to control plane) from the peer channel (agent to agent); a real partition is created by disconnecting an edge region from the control channel while the region keeps its peer channel; and recovery is handled by a local coordinator elected within the region over the peer channel, with no control plane. Roles are local to their region, which models edge data locality, so a partitioned control plane cannot move a role to another region (Section IV-C). The second is a real k3d/K3s cluster extended to 16 nodes across two physical machines connected over the internet by a mesh VPN, with roles packaged as pods that carry resource requests so that spare capacity is meaningful; it measures, on a real orchestrator, the quantities the fabric cannot capture: the operational accuracy of the certificate under real scheduling noise, robustness over a three-hour random chaos schedule of 180 node kills, and split-brain behavior under a real wide-area partition (Section IV-D). Failures are injected in three ways: stopping a node's container to model node loss; cutting a connection to create a real partition; and isolating a node or a cluster by blocking traffic to the control plane, using iptables on the K3s fabric and network disconnection on the container fabric, which keeps the node alive but out of contact, the split-brain condition. On the K3s platform a lease-based fencing layer freezes a node that has lost contact beyond a threshold before its replacement takes over. At the simulation layer, an augmenting-path solver (Edmonds-Karp) on the node-split graph serves as an optimal adversary, used to measure the gap to optimum, the self-stranding rate of the field, and the certificate under the capacitated model.

The three implementations differ in how decentralized they are, and each validates a different thing. The simulation implements the decentralized algorithm of Algorithm 1 exactly: each node keeps only local state, updates the field by one-hop exchange, and builds routes from neighborhood information; this is where the decentralized properties of the field algorithm are checked, including the number of rounds to convergence, the bytes exchanged, the quality relative to optimum, and the self-stranding rate. The container fabric raises the decentralization of execution from simulation to a real container system: when the control plane is partitioned, recovery is handled by a local coordinator elected inside the region over the peer channel, so it validates the core value, whether service is restored when there is no central control plane, with no control plane intervening. The K3s prototype does not run that peer recovery loop; instead a centralized controller reads cluster state through the Kubernetes API and calls kubectl, and it is used to measure, on a real orchestrator, the quantities the other two cannot capture: the accuracy of the certificate under real scheduling noise, split-brain, and the fencing layer. Failure detection on the K3s prototype currently relies on the centralized node-monitor-grace-period of Kubernetes, and a complete decentralized neighbor lease is the next step.

## IV. Results

### A. Certificate and Convergence Rate

The min-cut results match the analysis. On a 6 by 6 grid, as the number of spares grows from 4 to 16, the mean min-cut rises from 3.92 to 4.49 and $\kappa$ rises with it (Fig. 1). The communication cost of the field grows slowly with size. On a grid of diameter 14, Jacobi needs about 34 rounds, roughly 2.4 times the diameter. On random geometric graphs the ratio of rounds to diameter falls from 3.7 to 1.96 as $n$ grows. The $O(\log(1/\varepsilon)/\lambda_2)$ bound in Lemma 1 is loose for grids because $1/\lambda_2$ grows with the square of the diameter, yet in the experiments the round count is nearly linear in the diameter, so the result stays within the theoretical bound while being much better than the worst case. For data volume, the potential payload of the field for one recovery is about 0.9 KB per node (34 rounds times about 3.3 neighbors times 8 bytes). This is the potential payload alone, without telemetry, reservation and commit messages, or headers, so it is an optimistic lower bound rather than the full protocol traffic. What scales with the cluster is the slope, not the byte-for-byte figure: the per-node potential traffic is constant in size, $O(\deg)$, while a centralized decision must read the whole cluster state, about 98 KB on a small cluster and 398 KB on the 16-node fabric, close to linear in size. Each Jacobi round at a node is a weighted average over neighbors, costing $O(\deg)$ multiply-add operations, which is a few hundred operations per node for one recovery, under a millisecond on an embedded CPU, with $O(\deg)$ state.

The a-priori certificate $C_{rob}$ is computed exactly by brute force on the 6 by 6 grid: for each placement of spares we find the smallest failure set that removes full recovery of the demand, and $C_{rob}$ is that size minus one. For a nested sequence of placements $S_4 \subseteq S_6 \subseteq \dots \subseteq S_{16}$ (adding spares in farthest-point order), $C_{rob}$ is nondecreasing, at 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 4 as $m$ grows from 4 to 16, as Theorem 1 predicts, since adding a sink cannot lower the max-flow on any failure combination. When each $m$ instead uses a separately redesigned, non-nested spread, $C_{rob}$ can fall as $m$ grows (2, 2, 3, 3, 2, 4), which is not a violation of monotonicity but reflects placement quality, because a re-placement can leave a vulnerable pocket in the worst case. In both sequences $C_{rob}$ is below the mean $C(F,S)$, for example $C_{rob}=4$ against a mean of 4.95 at $m=16$, so the average recovers more but only $C_{rob}$ is the commitment for every failure combination. The brute force is combinatorial, needing $O(\sum_{i \le C_{rob}+1} \binom{n}{i})$ max-flow computations, which is feasible for a fabric of tens of nodes and confirmed to run at $n=16$; at production scale, cut enumeration, branch-and-bound, an integer program, or a fast conservative bound such as local connectivity is needed.

