### B. Decentralized Recovery

On grid graphs, the uncoordinated discrete algorithm drops sharply when failures arrive in clusters, whereas the field tracks both the centralized greedy method and the optimal solution (Fig. 2). The gap to optimum, measured directly against the augmenting-path solver in Section IV-E, is small. On non-uniform random geometric graphs the field even exceeds the greedy method: at $n=300$, $\kappa$ reaches 0.263 against 0.113, while each round uses only one-hop exchange. This does not mean the field is better than the optimal solution or an integer program; the greedy method must run a global search per source, and the integer program needs the whole system state. The field reaches near-equivalent quality through local exchange with a bounded number of rounds, so its advantage is coordination cost, not solution quality.

