### C. Control-Plane Partition: Decentralized Against Centralized Recovery

The central question for a decentralized self-healing mechanism is what happens when the central control plane itself disappears, which Section I argues is the only regime where a mechanism above the Kubernetes scheduler can add availability. To isolate that regime we built a container fabric (Section III-D) in which the partition, node death, and reassignment are all real operations.

The fabric has 15 agents, each an agent holding a set of roles under capacity $b=4$. Three agents are co-located with the control plane (region $s_0$, never partitioned, a control) and twelve form an edge region $s_1$ carrying 36 roles at about 75% of capacity. Roles are local: a role of $s_1$ can only run on an agent in $s_1$, which models edge data locality, so when $s_1$ loses the control plane the control plane cannot move a role to another region. Two virtual networks separate the control channel from the peer channel. A real partition is created by disconnecting all of $s_1$ from the control channel while the agents keep the peer channel, after which $k$ edge nodes are killed. Three recovery modes are compared on exactly the same failure schedule. The baseline relies only on the central control plane. The peer heuristic is a strong decentralized comparator: every surviving agent in $s_1$ independently reassigns orphaned roles over the peer channel to the least-loaded reachable peer, respecting the capacity $b$, but with no elected coordinator, no reservation, and no certificate. CertiHeal adds coordination and the certificate to that same local recovery: the surviving agents elect a local coordinator, the lowest-index survivor, which reassigns orphaned roles over the peer channel under a capacity-checked reservation and needs no control plane. The peer heuristic isolates what local recovery achieves on its own, so the contribution of the coordinator and the certificate is read off the difference between the two. In this degenerate star the certificate reduces to a capacity check: full recovery is feasible if and only if the surviving capacity of the region holds all roles, that is $(12-k)\,b \ge 36$, equivalently $k \le 3$. We sweep $k$ from 1 to 6, five seeds per level, and measure role coverage, the fraction of roles served by at least one live agent, together with the number of double-booked roles, those held by more than one live agent.

The three modes separate cleanly (Table 2, Fig. 3). With the control plane lost, the baseline cannot reassign the orphaned roles in the partitioned region because locality forbids moving them elsewhere: coverage falls linearly with the number of dead nodes, from 92.9% at $k=1$ to 57.1% at $k=6$, and does not recover during the partition. CertiHeal recovers locally over the peer channel and holds exactly 100% coverage across the whole region the certificate declares feasible ($k \le 3$), then tracks the remaining capacity beyond it, at 90.5%, 81.0%, and 71.4% for $k=4, 5, 6$. The peer heuristic recovers far more than the baseline, since it too acts locally, but it does not match CertiHeal in the feasible region: its mean coverage is 100%, 98.6%, and 96.7% for $k=1, 2, 3$ against CertiHeal's exact 100%, and unlike CertiHeal it varies across seeds, dropping to 35 of 42 roles in the worst feasible run.

More important than the coverage figure is the relation between the certificate and the outcome. Across all 30 CertiHeal runs (five seeds by six levels), the confusion matrix between "certificate says feasible" and "recovers fully" is $\text{TP}=15$, $\text{FP}=0$, $\text{TN}=15$, $\text{FN}=0$: whenever the certificate says feasible the system recovers fully, and whenever it says infeasible the system does not recover fully and does not over-promise. The false-safe rate, the probability of a hung role when the certificate declares success, is zero over the 15 feasible configurations. Because the outcome is deterministic in $k$ on this topology, the five seeds reproduce it exactly rather than sampling it independently, so this is the exact behavior of the capacity-checked reservation on the tested topology rather than a statistical estimate; with 15 feasible configurations a rule-of-three bound places the 95% upper limit on the false-safe rate at about 20%. This is a direct realization of the fail-closed property that Section IV-D states as the operational target: when the real constraints match the capacitated flow model, the model certificate becomes an operational commitment that does not fail. The recovery time of CertiHeal grows with the number of roles to reassign, from about 6 s at $k=1$ to about 27 s at $k=6$ (Fig. 3), the coordination time of a paced local-coordinator loop.

The peer heuristic exposes why coordination is needed for the certificate to hold. Lacking a reservation, two surviving agents can adopt the same orphaned role: across the 30 peer runs 32 roles were double-booked, up to 14 at $k=1$, whereas CertiHeal double-booked none. Those duplicate placements consume capacity, so the peer heuristic strands other roles and fails to recover fully in 2 of its 15 model-certified configurations, a 13% failure rate within the region the model certificate calls feasible (the peer heuristic issues no certificate of its own, so this is not a false-safe rate), against 0 of 15 for CertiHeal. Local recovery on its own therefore restores most of the coverage, but only the coordinated, capacity-checked reservation makes the certificate a commitment that holds and keeps each role on a single node. The baseline and CertiHeal coverage are deterministic across all five seeds, the baseline losing exactly $3k$ roles and CertiHeal recovering to the capacity ceiling of the region; the peer heuristic is the only condition that varies with the seed, because its outcome depends on the order in which the uncoordinated agents claim roles.

The reservation must also survive coordinator failure, not only steady state, so we inject a coordinator kill: after recovery begins we kill the acting coordinator, the lowest-index survivor, which orphans its own roles as well and forces a new coordinator to take over mid-episode. Across ten runs at $k=1$ and $k=2$, both within the certificate after the extra death, recovery completed to full 42 of 42 coverage in every run and double-booked no role, so the re-election together with the per-slot $(e,\text{slot})$ state recovers a partial commit without losing or duplicating an assignment, the coordinator-failure case of the state machine of Section III-B. The kill adds coordination time when it lands during a larger reassignment, from about 6 s at $k=1$ to about 27 s at $k=2$, because the new coordinator re-runs the episode and also places the dead coordinator's roles.

This fabric is a real container implementation of decentralized recovery, but it is a separate harness rather than Kubernetes; Section IV-D adds the validation on Kubernetes itself.

