### D. Validation on a Real Kubernetes Cluster: Operational Certificate, Split-Brain, and Fencing

The fabric of Section IV-C isolates the decentralized mechanism but runs on a separate harness. Two quantities appear only on a real orchestrator: the accuracy of the certificate under scheduling noise, and split-brain behavior under partition. We deploy the mechanism on a real k3d/K3s cluster (Section III-D), extended to a 16-node fabric across two physical machines connected over the internet by a mesh VPN (Tailscale, WAN latency 70 to 90 ms; three nodes on the first machine, thirteen on the second), running 64 roles.

On this platform we inject a chaos schedule of 180 random node kills over three hours, generated in sync on both machines from a shared seed: every 45 to 150 s one to three nodes are killed, occasionally up to seven at once, rejoining after 30 to 180 s. Three hours of continuous, random, and overlapping failure on real distributed infrastructure show that the mechanism operates at this scale and duration: the system is at full 64 of 64 roles for 93.6% of the time, with a median reassignment time of about 2 s that is nearly independent of the number of simultaneous failures (detection time remains bounded by the centralized node-monitor-grace-period of Kubernetes). The certificate is the quantity that no Kubernetes configuration emits, and its accuracy on the real cluster must be read at the level of failure episodes rather than sampling ticks. The run contained 52 episodes, each a contiguous window with at least one failed node, and the samples within an episode are strongly autocorrelated, so a per-tick accuracy is not an independent-sample estimate. Counting an episode as a hang when a role stays unplaceable for at least three consecutive samples, about the reassignment time, the episode-level confusion between the certificate verdict and the outcome is 42 true positives, 6 false-safe episodes, 0 true negatives, and 4 false negatives. Under the strictest definition, any pending role at all, the false-safe count rises to 17 of 48 within-certificate episodes. Roles become unplaceable when the failures exceed the certificate, up to 31 at the peak (Fig. 4).

The model certificate and its operational use must be separated. $C(F,S)$ is exact in the modeled capacitated flow network, and in the controlled fabric of Section IV-C, where every assignment is accepted by real capacity, its false-safe rate is zero. Read against a live orchestrator, the same quantity is an unreliable operational predictor: the episode-level false-safe rate runs from 12 percent at the three-sample threshold (6 of 48 within-certificate episodes) to 35 percent under the strictest definition, and the specificity is near zero because most episodes that exceed the certificate still recover without a sustained hang. The episode set is heavily imbalanced, 48 of 52 episodes lying within the certificate, so the certificate almost always predicts feasible and the four beyond-certificate episodes are too few to estimate specificity reliably; these numbers are a first estimate on a single three-hour schedule, and a schedule engineered to dwell beyond the certificate boundary, run several times with an episode-level confidence interval, is needed to characterize the infeasible class. Scheduling order and timing, which the flow model does not capture, drive this gap. Both results point the same way: reading $C(F,S)$ against live telemetry is not fail-closed, whereas issuing the bound only after a real capacity check accepts the whole assignment, as the fabric reservation does, produced no false-safe outcome in the fabric of Section IV-C. A controlled admission experiment on the same Kubernetes cluster confirms this and sharpens it. We make the workload heterogeneous, thirty role pods at three CPU and fifteen at nine (225 CPU on sixteen 32-CPU nodes), so a uniform-capacity certificate can over-count, then withdraw scheduling capacity by cordoning $L$ nodes and re-placing the whole workload, over eighteen episodes with $L$ from six to twelve. Three decision rules diverge. Reading the model slot-certificate is not fail-closed: it declares feasible in thirteen episodes and hangs in ten of them, a false-safe rate of 77 percent. Summing the real CPU requests is better but still over-promises, 50 percent, because real placement is bin-packing and a capacity sum ignores fragmentation, the nine-CPU pods that do not fit a partly filled node. Only the reservation that commits after the real placement accepts every pod is fail-closed: it declares feasible only when nothing stays pending, so its false-safe rate is zero by construction (fifteen true negatives, no false-safe). The proved object is the model certificate; the operational guarantee comes not from any capacity sum but from committing only what a real placement accepts, on the fabric of Section IV-C and now on Kubernetes.

A real wide-area partition also exposes a safety problem that stopping a container does not show. When the second machine is disconnected, its edge nodes stay alive and keep running their old roles while the control plane creates replacements, so during the partition window the number of concurrently running replicas doubles; for a stateful service, two writing replicas can corrupt data. We add a lease-based fencing layer: a node that has lost contact with the control plane beyond a lease threshold freezes itself and stops serving before its replacement takes over. Measured directly on the same partition, without fencing the isolated side serves throughout the partition (41 roles alongside 18 replacements), the concurrent-execution window lasts the full 161 s and is in principle unbounded in the length of the outage; with fencing the isolated side stops after the lease expires, and the window shrinks to about 49 s and then to zero for the rest of the partition (Fig. 5). The layer trades a short pause bounded by the lease threshold for a determinate upper bound on concurrent-execution exposure, in place of a window that grows with the outage.

