### E. Strong Algorithmic Adversary: Gap to Optimum and the Limit of Local Recovery

To position recovery quality against a named strong solver rather than only against a discrete algorithm, we implement a vertex-disjoint flow solver by augmenting paths (Edmonds-Karp) on the same node-split graph of the field: this solver reaches the maximum vertex-disjoint flow, equal to the min-cut, and is therefore an optimal ceiling. On an 8 by 8 grid with clustered failures the field tracks this ceiling at small $t$ but falls away as $t$ grows (Fig. 6). The fraction of configurations where the field falls below the optimum, self-stranding through sequential reservation, is 0% at $t \le 2$, rising to 33.0% at $t=6$ and 52.0% at $t=8$. The augmenting-path solver recovers these configurations and reaches the optimum, but must find a global flow: the mean number of edges traversed rises from 55 to 565 with $t$, while the field uses only about 32 to 37 rounds of one-hop exchange that is nearly constant. The field therefore trades a measurable gap to optimum for locality, and the augmenting-path solver is a strong adversary that exposes exactly that gap.

A second experiment tests the capacity model. The unit vertex-disjoint model assumes one role per node, while the testbed lets a node carry up to six pods. Splitting a node into an in-out pair with an inner edge of capacity $c$ makes the min-cut a certificate under the capacitated model. The certificate rises almost linearly with node capacity: means of 5.09, 10.28, 15.20, 20.50, and 30.30 for $c$ equal to 1, 2, 3, 4, and 6 (Fig. 6). At the testbed capacity of 6 the certificate is about 30, so the unit-capacity model underestimates the true repairability of the fabric by about six times. The model does not merely multiply capacity uniformly: in a controlled bridge example (six sources through two relay nodes to six spares), the certificate equals $\min$(bridge, bind constraint), and the min-cut tracks the tightest constraint, equal to the bridge link capacity when the link is the bottleneck (2, 4, 6, 6 for link capacities 2, 4, 6, 8) and equal to the relay node capacity when the node is the bottleneck. The certificate therefore reflects the actual, heterogeneous capacity constraint rather than being a mechanical consequence of multiplying capacity.

The guarantee of the fallback branch (Theorem 2) is tested directly. Running the nominal branch in parallel with an imperfect learned branch (noisy conductance) and taking $f_{out}=\max(f_\theta, f_0)$, on 500 clustered-failure configurations $f_{out} \ge f_0$ holds 100% by construction, and in 14.6% of the configurations the nominal branch serves more demand than the learned branch, so using the learned branch alone would be worse and the max is a real safeguard rather than a trivial one (the learned branch is better in only 2.8%). The mean gap from $f_{out}$ to the exact $C(F,S)$ is only 0.1 role, at the cost of running the field solve twice per episode.

