## V. Discussion

Across the simulations, the local field tracks centralized orchestrators while exchanging only with one-hop neighbors. The min-cut certificate provides a bound that neither reactive Kubernetes nor learned orchestrators emit. Proactive migration uses the meaning of the certificate: it turns a cluster of simultaneous failures into a sequence of events, which moves the system into an easier region of the $\kappa(t)$ curve. The learning component contributes only at the margin, and the reinforcement-learning variant is worse than the plain field; this supports letting the guarantees come from the structure of the problem rather than from learning.

The value of decentralized recovery appears only when the central control plane disappears (Section I): under a control-plane partition, the central scheduler cannot reach the edge region and the orphaned roles there stay down, while the peer-to-peer local recovery of CertiHeal-Edge restores them from intra-region communication alone and respects capacity (Section IV-C). A strong decentralized peer heuristic, added as a comparator, restores most of that coverage as well, which shows that it is local recovery, not the certificate, that beats a partitioned central scheduler; what the certificate and the coordinated reservation add on top is the commitment, full recovery throughout the certified-feasible region and zero double-booking, where the uncoordinated heuristic strands roles and places some on two nodes at once. The difference from the central baseline is about whether recovery depends on a central control plane, not about the quality of placing a single role. In the same experiment the fail-closed property of the certificate moves from a target to a measured outcome: issued as a capacity-checked reservation, the certificate did not declare success falsely on any tested scenario, and its lower operational accuracy on the real cluster (Section IV-D) measures the degradation that unmodeled scheduling noise adds rather than a flaw in the graph theorem.

Within the scope of this study, the results should be read under the following limitations.

The certificate rests on a classical min-cut, and if the mechanism reduces to a min-cost flow with potentials, any claim of being faster should be read as an empirical result; the core contribution remains the local computation, the operational reading, and the fallback. The field does not beat the optimal solution or an integer program. It is a local heuristic that self-strands at a rate that grows with the number of failures, reaching more than half the configurations at the largest tested load, while an augmenting-path solver reaches the optimum but must find a global flow (Section IV-E). The gap to optimum is the price of locality rather than a hidden defect, and the $\kappa$ improvements should be read against the gap to optimum and the strongest adversary, not against an uncoordinated discrete algorithm.

The failure model covers fail-stop only; Byzantine faults, where a node keeps running but returns wrong or dishonest results, and silent failures are out of scope. The testbed evaluation ran on a 16-node fabric across two physical machines over the internet, with a real network partition and three hours of random chaos (Sections IV-C, IV-D), but it is still smaller than a production deployment of hundreds of nodes, and the test load is lightweight busybox pods, so the absolute numbers reflect orchestration latency rather than real application startup.

The container fabric and the K3s prototype validate different things, as Section III-D sets out: the fabric runs the decentralized peer recovery under a real partition, while the K3s prototype runs a centralized controller and Kubernetes detection to reach the operational accuracy of the certificate and split-brain, which only real infrastructure exposes. Two limitations follow. The fabric is a separate harness rather than a production edge autonomy framework, so placing the decentralized agent on KubeEdge or OpenYurt with real workloads and traces is the next step; and a decentralized neighbor lease that replaces the centralized detection grace-period would shorten detection. The most stable value of the testbed remains the certificate, since no Kubernetes configuration emits this quantity.

The concurrent-execution window under partition is a real risk rather than a transient effect: for a stateful service, two writing replicas can corrupt data. The lease-based fencing layer freezes a node that has lost contact beyond the lease and stops it from serving, and measured on the same partition it shrinks the window from the whole outage down to an upper bound set by the lease, while still returning roles to a node when the partition heals (Section IV-D). Fencing only bounds the exposure at the lease threshold rather than removing the overlap instantly, so the write-safety guarantees should be read for stateless or idempotent load; reaching no overlap for stateful services needs a token fence that gates the activation of a replacement by the lease, or a remote power or network cutoff, which is a further step.

Three directions follow. The first is to place CertiHeal-Edge on a production edge autonomy framework such as KubeEdge or OpenYurt, with real workloads and traces and a token fence for stateful load; these frameworks provide disconnection autonomy but emit no certificate and perform no fencing, so the certificate and the fencing layer of CertiHeal-Edge sit above them rather than replace them. The second is to evaluate the learning component on larger graphs or longer distances, to determine whether the room the oracle reveals becomes a real benefit. The third is the redundancy-placement problem: how many spares are needed to guarantee repair of $t$ failures on a class of graphs. A result of that kind would turn the certificate from an observation tool into a tool for infrastructure design.

## VI. Conclusion

CertiHeal-Edge is a self-healing framework in which a decentralized field algorithm recovers failures from local data alone, emits a repairability certificate with a fallback, and can migrate roles before a node actually fails. The algorithm is evaluated in simulation; a real container implementation shows that decentralized recovery restores service when the central control plane is partitioned away from an edge region, exactly where a centralized scheduler cannot, with the capacity-checked reservation acting as a fail-closed check that did not declare success falsely on any tested configuration; and a prototype on real Kubernetes shows that reading the certificate directly against live telemetry is unreliable, while committing only what a real placement accepts is fail-closed there as on the fabric, and it validates the lease-based fencing layer under a wide-area partition. Because the guarantee is a property of capacitated flow, min-cut, and the electrical field rather than of a learned model, an operator can publish a design-time tolerance level $C_{rob}$ for simultaneous non-spare failures before any incident and check the per-scenario recovery witness at each incident, which turns best-effort self-healing into a managed and checkable service. The immediate next step is to carry the decentralized agent onto a production edge framework with real workloads and a token fence for stateful services, so that the certificate and the fencing layer run above an existing disconnection-autonomy substrate.

## Appendix A. Proof Sketches

Theorem 1 (certificate: tight value, monotonicity, and cheap lower bound). Work on the live graph $G_F=G[V\setminus F]$, and split each node $v$ into an in-node $v_{in}$ and an out-node $v_{out}$ joined by an edge of integer capacity $b_v$; each link also carries an integer capacity. The virtual super-source $\sigma_F$ emits the total demand $d(F)=\sum_{v\in F} d_v$ into the recovery boundary $B_F=N(F)\setminus F$, and each spare is a sink with capacity equal to the number of roles it can still accept. The maximum units of demand served at once then equals the integral max-flow from $\sigma_F$ to the sink set $S$ in that node-split network, and by the max-flow min-cut theorem equals the capacitated $\sigma_F$-$S$ min-cut [63]; this is the tight value of the certificate $C(F,S)$. When all capacities equal one, the result reduces to the vertex-disjoint form of Menger's theorem. Two further properties hold: the value does not decrease when spares are added, since adding a sink cannot lower the max-flow; and by weak duality any feasible flow of value $f$ does not exceed the max-flow, so $f$ is a valid lower bound on $C(F,S)$, and the flow the field builds gives a conservative lower bound computed inside the local field rounds. $\blacksquare$

Theorem 2 (fallback branch: per-scenario guarantee, not optimal). With fixed nominal conductance $c^0$, $c_{min}>0$ on the live graph $G_F$, and Dirichlet boundary $u=1$ on the virtual super-source $\sigma_F$, $u=0$ on $S$, the system $L_{c^0}\,u=b$ on $G_F$ has a unique solution on each connected component. Let $f_0(F,S)$ be the units of demand that the current-tracing and capacity-reservation procedure serves from the nominal solution for the exact scenario $(F,S)$. The procedure is greedy, so $f_0$ can be smaller than $C(F,S)$: sequential reservation can strand later sources even though a larger flow exists, the self-stranding rate grows with the number of simultaneous failures (Section IV-E), and closing the gap to optimum needs an augmenting-path mechanism. Because the gate at steps 8 and 9 takes $f_{out}=\max(f_\theta, f_0)$ and the nominal branch does not depend on the learned weights, the final result satisfies $f_{out}(F,S) \ge f_0(F,S)$ for every scenario, regardless of the learned policy. This is a per-scenario guarantee rather than a bound on an average probability. $\blacksquare$

Lemma 1 (field convergence, independent of the value of the learned weights). Consider the live graph $G_F$ restricted to edges with positive conductance; assume $c_{vw} \in [c_{min},c_{max}]$ with $c_{min}>0$, and that each connected component contains at least one Dirichlet boundary node, either a boundary node of $B_F$ carrying the source $\sigma_F$ or a spare in $S$. Step 5 is a Jacobi iteration for the weighted Laplace system with Dirichlet boundary, and the iteration matrix on the interior nodes is $M=D_c^{-1}A_c$, with $D_c$ the weighted-degree diagonal and $A_c$ the weighted adjacency. $M$ is nonnegative and sub-stochastic: rows adjacent to the boundary sum to less than one, purely interior rows sum to one. Because every interior communicating class reaches a deficient row, a boundary-adjacent node, the spectral radius $\rho<1$, handled per connected block if the subgraph is separated across the boundary. The iteration converges geometrically in the infinity norm at the interior nodes, and the number of rounds for an error of at most $\varepsilon$ is $O(\log(1/\varepsilon)/(1-\rho))$. With conductances uniformly bounded in $[c_{min},c_{max}]$, there is a uniform worst-case lower bound on $1-\rho$ expressed through the conductance ratio $c_{max}/c_{min}$ and the Dirichlet boundary geometry, independent of the specific weight configuration produced by $g_\theta$: on a grid of diameter $D$ the worst-case $1-\rho=\Theta(1/D^2)$, giving $O(D^2\log(1/\varepsilon))$ rounds, while empirically the round count is near-linear in $D$ (Section IV-A). The result is consistent with near-optimal distributed Laplacian solvers [16]. $\blacksquare$

Lemma 2 (asynchronous convergence). The Dirichlet Jacobi matrix $M$ of Lemma 1 is in general not a contraction in the uniform infinity norm, since purely interior rows sum to one. However, because $M$ is nonnegative, sub-stochastic, and each component reaches the boundary, there is a positive weight vector $w$ for which $M$ is a contraction in the weighted sup-norm associated with $w$, with contraction factor $\rho<1$. We use the partially asynchronous model of Bertsekas and Tsitsiklis with three schedule assumptions: each node is updated infinitely often; the gap between two consecutive updates of the same node is bounded above by $B$; and each update reads neighbor values at most $\Delta$ steps old. For a contraction in a weighted sup-norm, the corresponding convergence theorem guarantees that the iteration converges to the same fixed point, and the rate depends on $B$, $\Delta$, and the fairness of the schedule rather than on $\Delta$ alone; under these assumptions the time to reach error $\varepsilon$ is $O((B+\Delta)\log(1/\varepsilon)/(1-\rho))$. $\blacksquare$

## Supplementary Material

This material collects two mechanisms that sit outside the core guarantee of CertiHeal-Edge: an optional local learning head that tunes the field conductances, and remaining-useful-life proactive migration. Both are reported here so that the main text keeps the repairability certificate and the decentralized recovery as its central claims. All parameters are those of Table 1.

**S-A. Local learning policy (method).** The learning component is optional and only adjusts conductance from local features. A shallow GNN with $K=2$ to $3$ message-passing steps is trained offline on the features of each edge and its two endpoints; its message passing coincides with the relaxation steps of the field, following the unrolled-optimization approach [55]. At runtime each node infers on a CPU, with an A100 GPU used only for training, so locality is preserved. Its improvement and its limits are reported in Section S-C.

**S-B. Proactive migration via remaining-useful-life prediction.** RUL and anomaly scores computed at a node enter the source term of the field, so a role is moved off a node at risk of failing before that node stops. In the experiment, RUL_predict is modeled as the true RUL plus Gaussian noise with standard deviation $\sigma$. This is an oracle sensitivity analysis rather than an evaluation of a real AI predictor: it shows how sensitive the migration mechanism is to prediction quality, not that a specific model achieves that quality. Real RUL error is usually non-Gaussian, biased, drifting, and heavy-tailed, so the numbers here should be read as a function of prediction accuracy, and plugging in a real RUL model on public data is the next step. Because migrations run sequentially, they do not contend for spare capacity at the same time as a whole-cluster failure. Each point is averaged over 250 runs. For $\sigma \le 1.5$ the mechanism keeps almost all roles: 100% at $\sigma \le 1.0$ (250 of 250, a 95% lower bound of about 0.985 by the rule of three) and 99.9% at $\sigma = 1.5$. The reactive alternative, waiting for the failure and then repairing, keeps about 84% (0.84 to 0.85, a 95% interval of about $\pm 0.05$ at this sample size). The two break even at $\sigma \approx 2.4$; beyond that the prediction is too inaccurate and proactive migration becomes worse than reactive (Fig. S1).

[[FIG:fig5_proactive.png|Fig. S1. RUL-based proactive migration (blue) against the reactive alternative (red). The proactive mechanism keeps close to 100% of roles up to about $\sigma$ = 1.5 and breaks even at $\sigma \approx 2.4$.|470|313]]

**S-C. Local learning policy: improvement and limits.** The edge classifier reaches an AUC of 0.94 to 0.95 and nearly saturates at $K=2$ to $3$, showing that only a few hops of information are needed. The routing policy, however, improves little. With learned conductance the field closes only 15 to 34% of the gap to optimum, and in the feasible region typically only 15 to 20%. Once difficulty is controlled, this benefit does not grow with size. An oracle configuration, in which the edges on the optimal max-flow route are amplified by using the correct solution in advance, closes 33 to 100% of the gap. This is an upper bound on what conductance tuning alone can achieve and shows there is still room to research. We also tried learning the router directly with REINFORCE, but it performed worse than the plain field (Fig. S2). The cause is not over-smoothing but the limit of locality: the field already carries a follow-the-current bias that is near-optimal at the neighborhood level, so a GNN that sees only $K$ hops has little residual signal to exploit, and the room the oracle reveals lies in global optimal-route information that a local policy cannot infer from a few hops.

